{"id": "2026-09-15-news-supreme-court-blocks-trump-mail-ballot-rule", "date": "2026-09-15", "stream": "news", "headline": "Supreme Court blocks Trump mail-ballot rule", "summary": "The US Supreme Court refused to let the Trump administration enforce a USPS rule restricting mail-in ballots before the November midterms.", "url": "https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/9/15/us-supreme-court-rejects-trump-mail-ballot-restrictions-ahead-of-midterms", "source_domain": "aljazeera.com", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["politics"], "importance": 3, "display_body": "The US Supreme Court on Monday 14 September refused to let the Trump administration enforce a Postal Service rule restricting mail-in ballots, weeks before the November midterm elections. In an unsigned order the conservative-majority court declined to lift an injunction from US District Judge Indira Talwani, saying the administration was \"unlikely to succeed on the merits.\" The rule — adopted after a March executive order — would have required states to send the Postal Service lists of ballot recipients and let the agency reject ballots that did not match. Justices Samuel Alito and Clarence Thomas dissented; Trump-appointed Justice Brett Kavanaugh sided with the majority but signalled he could back the policy later. Alabama, North Carolina and Wisconsin have already begun mailing ballots. The order is an emergency-docket ruling and the underlying case continues in the lower court.", "why": "mail voting is heavily used in several states, disproportionately Democratic-leaning ones, so blocking the rule this close to the vote removes a last-minute change that election officials said would have been near-impossible to implement in time.", "thread_id": "2026-09-15-news-supreme-court-blocks-trump-mail-ballot-rule", "first_seen_date": "2026-09-15", "event_date": "2026-09-14", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["US Supreme Court", "Donald Trump", "US Postal Service"]}
{"id": "2026-09-15-news-us-adds-50-tariffs-on-canadian-goods", "date": "2026-09-15", "stream": "news", "headline": "US adds 50% tariffs on Canadian goods", "summary": "Washington applied 50% duties to 110 Canadian product categories while lifting others, escalating and recalibrating the trade war.", "url": "https://www.cbc.ca/news/business/tariffs-removed-added-9.7343778", "source_domain": "cbc.ca", "tier": "T2", "tags": ["ongoing"], "topics": ["economy", "geopolitics"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "A fresh round of US tariffs on 110 categories of Canadian goods took effect just after midnight on Tuesday 15 September, adding 50% duties on items from specific cheeses and motorboats to ATVs, furniture, aluminium, paper and steel beams. Washington simultaneously lifted tariffs on 10 other Canadian products — including salt, cement, sugar and toilet paper — saying it wanted to \"offset the burden to US commerce.\" According to a University of Ottawa trade-law analysis cited by CBC, the additions (~US$2.56bn of 2025 trade) and removals (~US$2.41bn) nearly cancel out, so the net expansion of tariff coverage is small; the recalibration suggests Washington is watching which costs reach US consumers. The moves follow the 50% tariffs Washington imposed on 22 August and Canada's retaliation on roughly 700 US product categories, in force since 8 September. A separate US ban on imports of Canadian alcohol, whey and large motorcycles, announced earlier this month, is set to begin 29 September.", "why": "Canada sends about three-quarters of its exports to the US, so even a marginally wider tariff net raises the risk of regional job losses — though the delayed import-ban date, economists told CBC, leaves room for a negotiated deal.", "thread_id": "2026-08-22-weekend-us-imposes-50-tariffs-on-canadian-goods", "first_seen_date": "2026-08-22", "event_date": "2026-09-15", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["United States", "Canada", "Donald Trump"]}
{"id": "2026-09-15-news-russian-frigate-fires-flares-at-danish-helicopte", "date": "2026-09-15", "stream": "news", "headline": "Russian frigate fires flares at Danish helicopter", "summary": "A Russian warship fired two flares at a Danish military helicopter over the Baltic near Gedser; Copenhagen summoned Russia's ambassador.", "url": "https://www.letemps.ch/articles/une-fregate-russe-tire-des-fusees-eclairantes-sur-un-helicoptere-militaire-danois-en-mer-baltique", "source_domain": "letemps.ch", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["geopolitics", "security"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "A Russian frigate fired two flares at a Danish military helicopter over the Baltic Sea off Gedser on Monday 14 September, the Danish armed forces said, one passing close to the aircraft. Copenhagen said the Fennec helicopter was on a routine photographic mission near the Russian warship, which was in international waters. Foreign Minister Lars Løkke Rasmussen called the incident \"totally unacceptable\" on Tuesday and summoned the Russian ambassador. The episode came as NATO jets shot down a drone that violated Lithuanian airspace near Kaunas, part of a run of airspace and maritime incidents along the alliance's eastern and northern flank.", "why": "direct, if non-lethal, Russian actions against NATO-member forces raise the risk of miscalculation and test how the alliance responds short of open conflict.", "thread_id": "2026-09-15-news-russian-frigate-fires-flares-at-danish-helicopte", "first_seen_date": "2026-09-15", "event_date": "2026-09-14", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Russia", "Denmark", "NATO"]}
{"id": "2026-09-15-news-wales-and-scotland-sign-cardiff-agreement", "date": "2026-09-15", "stream": "news", "headline": "Wales and Scotland sign Cardiff Agreement", "summary": "The First Ministers of Wales and Scotland signed the Cytundeb Caerdydd cooperation pact covering EU engagement, climate and the economy.", "url": "https://www.gov.wales/leaders-of-wales-and-scotland-set-out-plans-for-collaboration-with-signing-of-cytundeb-caerdydd-cardiff-agreement", "source_domain": "gov.wales", "tier": "T1", "tags": ["new source"], "topics": ["politics"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "The First Ministers of Wales and Scotland signed the \"Cytundeb Caerdydd – Cardiff Agreement\" on Monday 14 September, a cooperation pact between their two governments. Rhun ap Iorwerth (Plaid Cymru) and John Swinney (SNP) — both leading pro-independence parties now in power in Cardiff and Edinburgh — set out shared work on child poverty, cost-of-living pressures, fiscal arrangements, climate resilience and \"international engagement, including with the EU,\" as well as language, culture and sport. The document frames the collaboration as a way to \"strengthen democracy and improve outcomes\" in the two nations.", "why": "a formal Wales–Scotland compact, with an explicit EU-engagement strand, deepens intergovernmental coordination outside Westminster at a time when both devolved administrations are governed by parties that favour looser ties to London.", "thread_id": "2026-09-15-news-wales-and-scotland-sign-cardiff-agreement", "first_seen_date": "2026-09-15", "event_date": "2026-09-14", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Wales", "Scotland", "Cardiff Agreement"]}
{"id": "2026-09-15-news-epa-repeals-power-plant-emissions-limits", "date": "2026-09-15", "stream": "news", "headline": "EPA repeals power-plant emissions limits", "summary": "The US EPA moved to rescind most Biden-era greenhouse-gas limits on coal and gas power plants, announced by administrator Lee Zeldin.", "url": "https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/9/14/us-repeals-rules-limiting-greenhouse-gas-emissions-by-power-plants", "source_domain": "aljazeera.com", "tier": "T2", "tags": ["official PR"], "topics": ["politics", "science"], "importance": 1, "display_body": "The US Environmental Protection Agency moved to repeal most greenhouse-gas limits on coal- and gas-fired power plants, EPA administrator Lee Zeldin announced at a G20 energy ministers' meeting in Houston on Monday 14 September. The rollback targets the CO2 standards introduced under the Biden administration; Zeldin said earlier administrations had waged \"a war on coal\" and predicted Americans \"will experience falling electricity prices.\" Energy analysts generally attribute coal's long decline in US power generation more to cheap natural gas and renewables than to emissions rules, so the effect on prices and coal use is contested.", "why": "removing federal CO2 limits on the largest US industrial emitters would weaken a central plank of the country's climate policy just as global temperature records continue to be set.", "thread_id": "2026-09-15-news-epa-repeals-power-plant-emissions-limits", "first_seen_date": "2026-09-15", "event_date": "2026-09-14", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["EPA", "Lee Zeldin", "United States"]}
{"id": "2026-09-15-news-national-council-upholds-f-35-purchase", "date": "2026-09-15", "stream": "news", "headline": "National Council upholds F-35 purchase", "summary": "Switzerland's National Council reaffirmed the F-35 procurement and passed the CHF 3.4bn army budget despite the deal's collapsed fixed price.", "url": "https://www.srf.ch/news/schweiz/herbstsession-2026-trotz-fixpreis-fiasko-nationalrat-haelt-an-f-35-beschaffung-fest", "source_domain": "srf.ch", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["switzerland", "security"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "The National Council reaffirmed the F-35 fighter-jet purchase on Tuesday 15 September, approving the CHF 3.4 billion army-spending package (Armeebotschaft, the government's annual military-procurement bill) despite the collapse of the deal's fixed-price terms. Switzerland had understood its 2021 order for 36 US jets to carry a fixed price; Washington's position that the figure was not guaranteed has left the country facing substantial extra costs, the \"fixed-price fiasco\" of the debate's framing. The lower chamber nonetheless backed staying the course on the procurement as it worked through the session's defence business.", "why": "the vote keeps Switzerland committed to its single largest defence buy in decades even as the final bill — and who absorbs the overrun — remains unsettled.", "thread_id": "2026-09-15-news-national-council-upholds-f-35-purchase", "first_seen_date": "2026-09-15", "event_date": "2026-09-15", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Switzerland", "National Council", "F-35"]}
{"id": "2026-09-15-news-council-of-states-backs-mercosur-with-farm-aid", "date": "2026-09-15", "stream": "news", "headline": "Council of States backs Mercosur with farm aid", "summary": "The Council of States approved the EU-Mercosur deal while tying it to about half a billion francs in support for Swiss farmers.", "url": "https://www.srf.ch/news/wirtschaft/halbe-milliarde-fuer-bauern-staenderat-will-mercosur-abkommen-retten-mit-teurem-kompromiss", "source_domain": "srf.ch", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["switzerland", "economy"], "importance": 1, "display_body": "The Council of States backed the EU–Mercosur trade agreement on Monday 14 September but tied its approval to about half a billion francs in support for Swiss farmers. The upper chamber's compromise pairs a \"yes\" to the deal — which would open Swiss markets to more South American agricultural goods — with sustainability safeguards and the farm-aid package, an attempt to blunt agricultural opposition. Whether that trade-off would survive a likely popular referendum is, SRF notes, an open question.", "why": "Mercosur is one of Switzerland's most contested trade files, and the size of the farmer sweetener signals how much domestic resistance the government expects before any vote at the ballot box.", "thread_id": "2026-09-15-news-council-of-states-backs-mercosur-with-farm-aid", "first_seen_date": "2026-09-15", "event_date": "2026-09-14", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Switzerland", "Mercosur", "Council of States"]}
{"id": "2026-09-15-news-heat-pumps-reach-a-quarter-of-swiss-homes", "date": "2026-09-15", "stream": "news", "headline": "Heat pumps reach a quarter of Swiss homes", "summary": "A quarter of Swiss residential buildings had heat pumps at end 2025 per the Federal Statistical Office, though oil still heats about a third.", "url": "https://www.letemps.ch/articles/les-pompes-a-chaleur-supplantent-progressivement-le-mazout-dans-les-batiments-suisses", "source_domain": "letemps.ch", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["switzerland", "economy"], "importance": 1, "display_body": "A quarter of Switzerland's residential buildings were equipped with heat pumps at the end of 2025, according to Federal Statistical Office (BFS/OFS) building statistics reported this week. Heating oil nonetheless remains the single most common energy source, in about 33% of dwellings, as the shift away from fossil heating continues gradually rather than abruptly.", "why": "building heat is a large share of Swiss emissions and household energy bills, so the pace at which heat pumps displace oil is a concrete measure of how the country's energy transition is landing in people's homes.", "thread_id": "2026-09-15-news-heat-pumps-reach-a-quarter-of-swiss-homes", "first_seen_date": "2026-09-15", "event_date": null, "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Switzerland", "Federal Statistical Office"]}
